 by systematically testing common words, phrases, and variations. It splits the password into a dictionary of common words and uses a sliding window to try the next word in that list. This makes it much faster than brute-forcing a single password.

```
#include <string>
#include <string.h>
#include <string.h>

int main() {
    int n = 10;
    char word[100];
    int current = 0;
    int pos = 0;

    while (current < n) {
        if (current >= pos) {
            current = pos + 1;
            pos = pos + pos;
        }
        current = current - pos;

        // Create a list of words to test
        for (int i = 1; i < n; i++) {
            word[i] = "word";
            pos[i]++;
        }

        // Try a password starting from the first word
        for (int i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {
            if (current >= pos) {
                // Try the next word in the list
                if (current >= pos + i) {
                    word[i] = "word";
                    pos[i] = pos + i;
                }
                pos = pos + i;
            }
        }

        pos = pos + 1;
    }

    return 0;
}
```

This approach keeps the complexity low for a single password. If the password is short, you can shrink the window to 10 or 20 characters. For longer passwords, you can bump the window up to 50 or 100 characters. The logic is simple, but the speed comes from how quickly it cycles through the word list.

